Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedengtest.shoddy — test program for seedeng.shoddy's calculus
Rem words, and for the engine bridge underneath them
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the cuttletest / reckonertest / seedtest pattern:
Rem it prints what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion
Rem that fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS GRADED HERE, and what is not. seedeng.shoddy's several
Rem hundred arithmetic words are eng.shoddy's own known-answer suite's
Rem business (tst/eng.shoddy) plus the conversions each wraps. What is
Rem graded here is the part that was deferred and has just been built:
Rem the eight words that take a PROGRAM rather than a number, and the
Rem two pieces of reckoner.shoddy they stand on —
Rem
Rem   * RckPureOver, the body kind that hands a registered word the state
Rem     its program has to be run against, and
Rem   * RckProgFn / RckProgTry, which turn a [ ... ] cell into a
Rem     [ Number -- Number ] and say honestly when they cannot.
Rem
Rem THE PAIRING IS THE THING TO TEST. RckProgFn is total and answers 0
Rem where the program will not evaluate, so the whole safety of the eight
Rem words is that RckProgTry was asked first at every point the machine
Rem is going to use. Two sections below exist only for that: one proves
Rem a bad program is refused rather than silently integrated as zero, and
Rem one proves a program can still see the session it was typed in —
Rem a word defined a line earlier, a register stored a line earlier —
Rem because a bridge that cannot do that is not worth the engine change.
Rem
Rem Every refusal is walked in the seed suite's two-part shape: the line
Rem refuses for the stated reason with the state as it was, AND a further
Rem line evaluates afterwards. Only the second half proves the mill
Rem lived.
Rem
Rem Run: bin\mill run tst\seedengtest.shoddy      (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/cuttle.shoddy"
Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedeng.shoddy"
Rem Folded only for the fit section below, and onto a state of its own:
Rem a fit is a dict, and DGET is how a session actually reads one, so
Rem reading it any other way would test something nobody types.
Include "../machines/seeds/seeddict.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading an answer ------------------------------------------------

Def Ran(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Def Runs(st As RckState, lines As List Of String) As RckState
    Fold(lines, st, Ran)

Def Bad(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def Why(st As RckState, line As String) As String
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            why
        Case Else
            ""

Def Text(st As RckState) As String
    Join(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st))), " | ")

Def Deep(st As RckState) As Number
    CutDepth(RckStack(st))

Def TopNum(st As RckState) As Number
    If Deep(st) < 1 Then
        0 - 999999
    Else
        NumOf(First(CutCells(RckStack(st))))

Def NumOf(c As Cell) As Number
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            x
        Case Else
            0 - 999999

Rem A numeric derivative is accurate to about eight digits and a second
Rem difference to about four, so the tolerance here is the machine's own
Rem stated accuracy rather than an exactness nothing promises.
Def Tol() As Number
    0.0001

Def Near(a As Number, b As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= Tol()

Def Got(st As RckState, line As String) As Number
    TopNum(Ran(st, line))

Def Q(s As String) As String
    Chr(34) & s & Chr(34)

Def Refuses(st As RckState, line As String, part As String) As Boolean
    Bad(st, line) And Instr(Why(st, line), part) > 0 And Text(Ran(st, line)) = Text(st) And Text(Ran(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")) = Text(Ran(st, "1 1 +"))

Def Holds(lines As List Of String, sub As String) As Boolean
    Any(lines, Fn(l) => Instr(l, sub) > 0)

Def Main()
    Let s0 = RckSeedEng(RckNew())
    Let g = Ran(s0, "1 2")

    Print("--- the eight words are registered, pure, and carry their metadata ---")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGDERIV") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGDERIV2"), "THE TWO DERIVATIVES ARE WORDS")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGINTEGRATE") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGSUMOF") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGPRODOF"), "AND THE INTEGRAL, THE SUM AND THE PRODUCT")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGZEROOF") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMINOF") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMAXOF"), "AND THE THREE BRACKETED SEARCHES")
    Rem R3.5(a): ENGNEWTONOF can abort under its own derivative going to
    Rem zero and nothing can pre-flight that, so it is absent ON PURPOSE.
    Rem An assertion, not a comment, because "we left it out deliberately"
    Rem is exactly the kind of decision a later pass quietly undoes.
    Assert(Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGNEWTONOF"), "AND ENGNEWTONOF IS DELIBERATELY NOT ONE OF THEM")
    Assert(Holds(RckHelp(s0, "ENGDERIV"), "touches nothing"), "A WORD THAT RUNS A PROGRAM STILL ANSWERS FOR ITSELF, AS MAP DOES")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordEffect(e) <> "" And WordDesc(e) <> ""), "NO WORD IS WITHOUT AN EFFECT LINE AND A DESCRIPTION")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Length(RckHelp(s0, WordName(e))) = 3), "AND HELP ANSWERS FOR EVERY ONE OF THEM")

    Print("--- the derivatives, against derivatives known by hand ---")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP * ] 3 ENGDERIV"), 6), "D/DX OF X^2 AT 3 IS 6")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP DUP * * ] 2 ENGDERIV"), 12), "D/DX OF X^3 AT 2 IS 12")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ SIN ] 0 ENGDERIV"), 1), "D/DX OF SIN AT 0 IS 1, IN RADIANS")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ EXP ] 0 ENGDERIV"), 1), "AND OF EXP AT 0 IS 1")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ ] 3 ENGDERIV"), 1), "THE EMPTY PROGRAM IS THE IDENTITY, WHOSE DERIVATIVE IS 1")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP DUP * * ] 2 ENGDERIV2"), 12), "AND THE SECOND DERIVATIVE OF X^3 AT 2 IS 12")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP * ] 5 ENGDERIV2"), 2), "AND OF X^2 ANYWHERE IS 2")

    Print("--- the integral, the range sum and the range product ---")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP * ] 0 3 100 ENGINTEGRATE"), 9), "THE INTEGRAL OF X^2 FROM 0 TO 3 IS 9")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ ] 0 2 2 ENGINTEGRATE"), 2), "AND SIMPSON IS EXACT ON A LINE AT THE SMALLEST EVEN N")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ SIN ] 0 PI 100 ENGINTEGRATE"), 2), "AND THE INTEGRAL OF SIN OVER HALF A TURN IS 2")
    Assert(Got(s0, "[ ] 1 10 ENGSUMOF") = 55, "THE WHOLE NUMBERS 1 TO 10 SUM TO 55")
    Assert(Got(s0, "[ DUP * ] 1 4 ENGSUMOF") = 30, "AND THEIR SQUARES TO 4 SUM TO 30")
    Assert(Got(s0, "[ ] 1 5 ENGPRODOF") = 120, "AND 1 TO 5 MULTIPLY TO 5 FACTORIAL")
    Rem lo above hi is an empty range, not an error, and the empty sum and
    Rem the empty product are the identities of their operations.
    Assert(Got(s0, "[ ] 5 1 ENGSUMOF") = 0, "AN EMPTY RANGE SUMS TO 0")
    Assert(Got(s0, "[ ] 5 1 ENGPRODOF") = 1, "AND MULTIPLIES TO 1")

    Print("--- the bracketed searches ---")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP * 2 - ] 0 2 ENGZEROOF"), Sqr(2)), "THE ROOT OF X^2 - 2 IN [0,2] IS THE SQUARE ROOT OF 2")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ 1 - ] -5 5 ENGZEROOF"), 1), "AND OF X - 1 IS 1")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ 1 - DUP * ] -3 4 ENGMINOF"), 1), "(X-1)^2 IS LEAST AT 1")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "[ DUP * 0 SWAP - ] -3 4 ENGMAXOF"), 0), "AND -X^2 IS GREATEST AT 0")

    Print("--- the program is run against the WHOLE session, not a fresh one ---")
    Rem The reason RckPureOver exists. A program that cannot name a word
    Rem the user defined, or read a register they stored, is a program
    Rem nobody would type twice.
    Let u = Ran(s0, ": SQ DUP * ;")
    Assert(Near(Got(u, "[ SQ ] 3 ENGDERIV"), 6), "A PROGRAM MAY NAME A WORD DEFINED A LINE EARLIER")
    Let r = Ran(s0, "5 " & Q("K") & " STO")
    Assert(Near(Got(r, "[ " & Q("K") & " RCL * ] 1 ENGDERIV"), 5), "AND MAY RCL A REGISTER STORED A LINE EARLIER")
    Rem And what the program changes does NOT come home: a solver calling
    Rem it two hundred times must not leave two hundred registers behind.
    Let w = Ran(s0, "[ DUP * 9 " & Q("Z") & " STO ] 0 4 ENGMINOF")
    Assert(Bad(w, Q("Z") & " RCL"), "WHILE A REGISTER STORED INSIDE THE PROGRAM DOES NOT SURVIVE IT")
    Assert(Length(RckDict(Ran(s0, "[ DUP * ] 3 ENGDERIV"))) = Length(RckDict(s0)), "AND THE DICTIONARY COMES BACK THE SIZE IT WAS")

    Print("--- a bad program is refused, never silently taken as zero ---")
    Rem This is the section the whole design turns on. RckProgFn answers 0
    Rem where the program will not evaluate, so if the pre-flight were
    Rem missing each of these would return a confident wrong number
    Rem instead of refusing — [ NOSUCHWORD ] 0 3 100 ENGINTEGRATE would be
    Rem 0, not an error.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ NOSUCHWORD ] 3 ENGDERIV", "unknown word NOSUCHWORD"), "A PROGRAM NAMING A WORD THAT DOES NOT EXIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ NOSUCHWORD ] 0 3 100 ENGINTEGRATE", "unknown word NOSUCHWORD"), "AND THE SAME UNDER THE INTEGRAL, WHERE THE SILENT ANSWER WOULD HAVE BEEN 0")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ NOSUCHWORD ] 1 10 ENGSUMOF", "unknown word NOSUCHWORD"), "AND UNDER THE RANGE SUM")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ NOSUCHWORD ] 0 2 ENGZEROOF", "unknown word NOSUCHWORD"), "AND UNDER THE BRACKETED SEARCH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP ] 3 ENGDERIV", "needs its program to leave one value, it left 2"), "A PROGRAM THAT LEAVES TWO CELLS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DROP ] 3 ENGDERIV", "needs its program to leave one value, it left 0"), "AND ONE THAT LEAVES NONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DROP " & Q("x") & " ] 3 ENGDERIV", "a program leaving one NUMBER"), "AND ONE THAT LEAVES SOMETHING THAT IS NOT A NUMBER")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "3 3 ENGDERIV", "needs a PROGRAM, got NUMBER"), "AND AN ARGUMENT THAT IS NOT A PROGRAM AT ALL")
    Rem A program that is fine at some points and refuses at others: LN is
    Rem undefined at and below zero, so a bracket straddling it is caught
    Rem by the sample rather than quietly weighted with zeros.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ LN ] -1 2 ENGZEROOF", "LN has no answer at or below zero"), "AND ONE THAT REFUSES ONLY ON PART OF THE BRACKET IT WAS GIVEN")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ 1 0 / ] 0 2 ENGMINOF", "cannot divide by zero"), "AND ONE THAT REFUSES BY ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN BY NAME")
    Rem The engine's own refusals travel out unchanged, so the reason a
    Rem session reads is the reason the token gave, not a seed's paraphrase
    Rem of it.
    Assert(Why(g, "[ NOSUCHWORD ] 3 ENGDERIV") = "unknown word NOSUCHWORD", "AND THE REASON IS THE ENGINE'S OWN WORDS, NOT A PARAPHRASE")

    Print("--- the guards on the numbers, as against the program ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * ] 0 3 7 ENGINTEGRATE", "N MUST BE AN EVEN WHOLE NUMBER OF 2 OR MORE"), "SIMPSON WITH AN ODD N, WHICH WOULD WEIGHT THE WRONG NODES")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * ] 0 3 0 ENGINTEGRATE", "N MUST BE AN EVEN WHOLE NUMBER OF 2 OR MORE"), "AND WITH NO INTERVALS AT ALL, WHICH WOULD DIVIDE BY THEM")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * ] 0 3 4.5 ENGINTEGRATE", "N MUST BE AN EVEN WHOLE NUMBER OF 2 OR MORE"), "AND WITH A FRACTIONAL COUNT OF THEM")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ ] 1 2.5 ENGSUMOF", "LO AND HI MUST BOTH BE WHOLE NUMBERS"), "A RANGE WITH A FRACTIONAL END, WHICH RANGE WOULD WALK PAST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ ] 1.5 5 ENGPRODOF", "LO AND HI MUST BOTH BE WHOLE NUMBERS"), "AND ONE WITH A FRACTIONAL START")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * 2 + ] 0 2 ENGZEROOF", "DOES NOT CHANGE SIGN ACROSS THAT BRACKET"), "BISECTION ON A BRACKET WITH NO ROOT IN IT, WHICH WOULD ANSWER AN ENDPOINT")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * ] 4 1 ENGMINOF", "LO MUST BE BELOW HI"), "AND GOLDEN SECTION ON AN INVERTED BRACKET, WHICH WOULD WALK OUTWARDS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "[ DUP * ] 2 2 ENGZEROOF", "LO MUST BE BELOW HI"), "AND EITHER ON A BRACKET OF NO WIDTH")

    Print("--- the statistics words are spelled the way eng.shoddy spells them ---")
    Rem Seven of these used to carry an extra S — ENGSMEAN for EngMean,
    Rem ENGSMIN for EngMinimum — which disambiguated against MEAN and MIN,
    Rem a collision the ENG prefix had already prevented. seedfin carried
    Rem the same seven and they move together, because a session that
    Rem learns one set has learned the other.
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGMEAN") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGVAR") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMEDIAN"), "ENGMEAN, ENGVAR AND ENGMEDIAN ARE THE MACHINE'S OWN NAMES")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGQUANTILE") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMINIMUM") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMAXIMUM") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGRANGEOF"), "AND SO ARE THE OTHER FOUR")
    Assert(Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSMEAN") And Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSVAR") And Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSMEDIAN"), "THE S-FORMS ARE GONE RATHER THAN ALIASED")
    Assert(Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSQUANTILE") And Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSMIN") And Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSMAX") And Not RckKnows(s0, "ENGSRANGE"), "AND ALL SEVEN OF THEM ARE")
    Rem ENGMINOF and ENGMAXOF are the bracketed searches above and are a
    Rem different question from the smallest value in a list. Both spellings
    Rem stay, and this is the assertion that says the rename did not eat one.
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGMINOF") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGMAXOF"), "AND THE BRACKETED SEARCHES ARE STILL THEIR OWN WORDS")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } ENGMEAN"), 2.5), "AND THE WORDS STILL ANSWER: THE MEAN")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 2 4 4 4 5 5 7 9 } ENGVAR"), 32 / 7), "THE SAMPLE VARIANCE, DIVIDING BY N-1")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 2 4 4 4 5 5 7 9 } ENGVARP"), 4), "AND THE POPULATION ONE BESIDE IT, WHICH NEVER HAD AN S")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 3 1 2 } ENGMINIMUM"), 1), "THE MINIMUM")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 3 1 2 } ENGMAXIMUM"), 3), "THE MAXIMUM")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 3 1 2 } ENGRANGEOF"), 2), "AND THE RANGE BETWEEN THEM")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } 0 ENGQUANTILE"), 1), "THE ZEROTH QUANTILE IS THE SMALLEST VALUE")
    Assert(Near(Got(s0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } 1 ENGQUANTILE"), 4), "AND THE FIRST IS THE LARGEST")

    Print("--- ENGFITOF: least squares, and the four models by name ---")
    Let f0 = RckSeedDict(s0)
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGFITOF"), "THE FIT IS IN THE DICTIONARY")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ENGLINEAR") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGLOGMODEL") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGEXPMODEL") And RckKnows(s0, "ENGPOWERMODEL"), "AND ALL FOUR MODELS ARE WORDS, NOT NUMBERS TO REMEMBER")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } { 3 5 7 9 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF " & Q("slope") & " DGET"), 2), "A STRAIGHT LINE'S SLOPE")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } { 3 5 7 9 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF " & Q("intercept") & " DGET"), 1), "ITS INTERCEPT")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 3 4 } { 3 5 7 9 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF " & Q("rfit") & " DGET"), 1), "AND AN EXACT FIT CORRELATES AT ONE")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 4 } { 1 4 16 } ENGPOWERMODEL ENGFITOF " & Q("slope") & " DGET"), 2), "THE POWER MODEL RECOVERS THE EXPONENT OF y = x^2")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 4 } { 1 4 16 } ENGPOWERMODEL ENGFITOF " & Q("intercept") & " DGET"), 1), "AND ITS COEFFICIENT")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 4 } { 0 0.6931471805599453 1.3862943611198906 } ENGLOGMODEL ENGFITOF " & Q("slope") & " DGET"), 1), "THE LOGARITHMIC MODEL RECOVERS y = ln x")
    Assert(Near(Got(f0, "{ 1 2 3 } { 2 4 8 } ENGEXPMODEL ENGFITOF " & Q("slope") & " DGET"), Log(2)), "AND THE EXPONENTIAL ONE RECOVERS THE RATE OF A DOUBLING")

    Print("--- ENGFITOF's guards, including the two eng.shoddy does not state ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 } { 1 2 3 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "THEY MUST PAIR UP"), "LISTS OF DIFFERENT LENGTHS, WHICH ZIPWITH WOULD HAVE SILENTLY TRUNCATED")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 } { 2 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "NEEDS AT LEAST 2 POINTS"), "ONE POINT, WHERE THE VARIANCE DIVIDES BY N-1 AND N IS ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } { } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "NEEDS AT LEAST 2 POINTS"), "AND NO POINTS AT ALL")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } { 1 2 3 } 99 ENGFITOF", "NOT A MODEL"), "A MODEL NUMBER THAT IS NOT ONE, WHICH ENGFITOF WOULD ABORT ON")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 0 1 2 } { 1 2 3 } ENGLOGMODEL ENGFITOF", "NEEDS EVERY X ABOVE ZERO"), "A LOGARITHMIC FIT THROUGH X = 0")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } { 0 1 2 } ENGEXPMODEL ENGFITOF", "NEEDS EVERY Y ABOVE ZERO"), "AN EXPONENTIAL FIT THROUGH Y = 0")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ -1 2 3 } { 1 2 3 } ENGPOWERMODEL ENGFITOF", "NEEDS EVERY X ABOVE ZERO"), "AND A POWER FIT THROUGH A NEGATIVE X")
    Rem The two nobody wrote down: LinFit divides by Var(xs) and Correl
    Rem divides by StdDev(xs) * StdDev(ys), so a list that does not vary
    Rem divides by zero and the runtime ends the session.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 2 2 2 } { 1 2 3 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "EVERY X IS THE SAME"), "A VERTICAL LINE, WHICH DIVIDES BY VAR(XS)")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } { 2 2 2 } ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "EVERY Y IS THE SAME"), "AND A FLAT ONE, WHERE THE CORRELATION DIVIDES BY A SPREAD OF ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } " & Q("no") & " ENGLINEAR ENGFITOF", "needs a LIST, got STRING"), "AND A STRING WHERE THE Y VALUES SHOULD BE")

    Print("--- nothing above cost the session anything ---")
    Rem Top of stack first, as CutCells renders it: the 2 the sum just
    Rem made, then the 2 and the 1 that were under it all along.
    Assert(Text(Ran(g, "1 1 +")) = "2 | 2 | 1", "THE STACK IS STILL THERE AND STILL EVALUATING")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
